From d564181f7eee6a1dab43690759a46e0a429e1063 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 13:47:28 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] feat(llm): add a provider-neutral capability vocabulary
 for tier hints
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1a of the model-agnostic work. The per-feature admin dropdown has to
offer the operator *something* as "which class of model should this feature
use", and today every available name is a vendor product ("haiku", "sonnet-5",
"opus-5", "gemini", "tera", "luna") or a complexity label whose vendor is
implicit ("complex" is Anthropic, "medium" is OpenAI). A deployment that has
configured only OpenRouter and Ollama should not have to pick an Anthropic
product name for a feature that will never touch Anthropic.

Adds six provider-neutral names — fast / balanced / expert / vision /
long-context / local-only — across all three tier vocabularies so they mean
the same thing wherever a code path resolves them:

  models/model_router.py  _HINT_MAP
  core/config.py          AINXT_TIER_MAP
  core/model_registry.py  _SDLC_STAGE_HINTS_ALLOWED + cli_model_for_tier

STRICTLY ADDITIVE: not one existing key is repointed. The vocabulary was first
drafted as a re-aliasing ("simple" -> "fast", "medium" AND "complex" ->
"balanced") described as alias-only and therefore safe. Checking it against
_dispatch() shows it is not:

  * "simple" dispatches to the IN-HOUSE GPU (_try_local_simple), not to a
    cheap cloud model. Folding it into "fast" would have moved its 17 call
    sites off-prem — a cost AND a privacy regression.
  * "medium" (_try_openai_coding, OpenAI) and "complex" (_try_claude_sonnet,
    Anthropic) are different VENDORS, not different capability levels. Folding
    both into "balanced" would have silently switched vendor for 39 call sites.

So the mapping runs one way only: a capability name resolves to the tier that
best matches it today, and the legacy names keep working untouched.
_dispatch()/_dispatch_stream() are not modified at all.
tests/core/test_capability_vocabulary.py snapshots every pre-existing hint and
asserts it still resolves to the same tier; if that snapshot ever needs editing
to pass, the change under review is not alias-only.

Named "expert" rather than the "deep" originally proposed for the
highest-capability slot: "deep" is already a live key mapping to TIER_DEEP
(GPT-5-5), so repointing it to TIER_SOLUTION (Opus) would change vendor for
existing callers — the same mistake. Also deliberately did NOT add an
"opus-4-6" alias despite it looking like a gap: claude-opus-4-6 is retired and
sits in BLOCKED_MODELS, so the alias would point callers at a blocked model,
and no production call site uses it.

Two places needed more than a map entry:

- cli_model_for_tier returns an unrecognised hint VERBATIM as a concrete model
  id, so an unmapped capability name would have been dispatched as a model
  literally called "balanced". Each name is aliased onto an existing role so it
  also keeps honouring that role's SDLC_TIER_<TIER>_MODEL override.
  "local-only" short-circuits to the locally served model — it is a posture,
  not a role, and must never resolve to a cloud model id.
- gateway.py's _CLAUDE_TOOL_HINTS (and the IDE dispatch tuple) decide which
  hints route tool-calls to Anthropic. fast/balanced/expert resolve to
  Anthropic tiers, so without them a feature assigned "balanced" would have
  had its tool calls served by OpenAI while the same feature assigned "claude"
  got Anthropic — exactly the vendor leak this vocabulary removes.
  long-context (OpenAI), vision (Gemini) and local-only are correctly absent.

sdlc_stage_hint's ENABLE_OPUS downgrade now covers "expert" as well as
"solution" — both resolve to TIER_SOLUTION, so the kill-switch would otherwise
be bypassable by naming the capability instead of the tier.

Incidentally fixes two long-baselined failures in
tests/agents/test_gateway_passthrough_logic.py: its reference copy of
_CLAUDE_TOOL_HINTS had drifted (still listed the retired "opus-4-6", missing
"opus-5"/"sonnet-5"), which also meant one parametrised case was asserting a
routing decision the gateway has never made. Both references now match the
source. The remaining failure in that file is unrelated and still baselined.

Also removes an unreachable raise in model_router: the "hard-fail on empty
_HINT_MAP keys" check sat directly after the comprehension that strips every
falsy key, so it could never fire — and a blank model env var is routine on an
admin-configured deployment, so raising would have been wrong regardless.

71 new tests in tests/core/. No new failures against
scripts/ci/known_failures.txt; all release checks pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
