From adeaa4a57767cf342e0129222fcc0f515b88d4b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:06:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] =?UTF-8?q?feat(llm):=20make=20feature=20=E2=86=92?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20model=20assignment=20a=20first-class,=20admin-editable=20con?=
 =?UTF-8?q?cept?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1b of the model-agnostic work, and the layer the whole ask depends on.
llm_providers/llm_models already let an admin register arbitrary providers and
models at runtime, and dept_model_permissions/user_model_permissions already
gate WHO may use a model — but nothing mapped "the Coach module" or "Skills
generation" to one. That was a tier-name literal compiled into each call site,
so changing it meant editing Python and redeploying.

  db/models.py             FeatureRegistry + FeatureModelConfig
  db/migrate.py            Part AD1 — both tables, seeded, + the telemetry column
  core/feature_registry.py 31 declared features across 8 categories
  core/feature_model_resolver.py  the resolution chain
  routers/feature_model_config_router.py  admin CRUD
  ai-ui/.../FeatureModelConfig.jsx        the admin screen

No router change was needed. ModelRouter.route() step 1a already resolves an
arbitrary admin-registered model_id passed as model_hint, before _HINT_MAP, so
the resolver returns a plain string and a migrated call site is a one-argument
substitution:

  model_router.generate(prompt, model_hint="complex")
  model_router.generate(prompt, model_hint=resolve_feature_model(
      "skills.generate", default="complex"))

Passing the call site's CURRENT literal as `default=` is what makes the
migration safe to land file-by-file: until an admin assigns something the
resolver returns exactly what the call site would have used. The resolver never
raises — an unknown feature, a DB outage, a deleted model or a disabled
provider all degrade to `default`, because this now sits on the path of every
LLM call in the platform.

Precedence, highest first: the privacy floor (NOT overridable by feature
config); AINXT_FEATURE_MODEL_<KEY> break-glass, mirroring the proven
SDLC_MODEL_<STAGE> pattern; the org's row; the platform-wide row; the feature's
declared default_capability; the call site's literal.

Three design points worth stating:

- default_capability is a LIVE fallback, consulted BEFORE the call site's
  literal, so it is only seeded where the capability resolves to the EXACT SAME
  tier as the literal those sites pass today (local-only==simple, fast==haiku,
  balanced==complex, expert==solution). Features whose sites use "medium"/"gpt"
  (TIER_MEDIUM, OpenAI) are deliberately left at None: no capability name maps
  to TIER_MEDIUM, so seeding the nearest one would have switched them from
  OpenAI to Anthropic the moment this table landed. test_feature_registry.py
  enforces both halves of that rule.

- Granularity is one key per (module, capability class), not per module.
  agents/advanced_reasoning.py has 6 call sites on the local tier and 4 on the
  Anthropic workhorse; collapsing them would mean assigning a cloud model to
  "advanced reasoning" silently moved the six cheap local passes onto it too.

- feature_model_config stores the llm_models UUID, not the model_id string, so
  renaming a model in the admin screen cannot orphan the assignment. That
  needed a new get_model_by_uuid() — the existing get_model() keys on the API
  model_id string, which is unique only per-provider.

Validation is server-side as well as in the UI, because UI filtering is
bypassable by any API client and both failure modes are silent at runtime: a
text-only model on a vision feature returns nonsense, and a cloud model on a
CONFIDENTIAL feature is overridden on-premise by route()'s privacy floor, so
the admin believes an assignment took effect that never can. Capability checks
are one-sided (a sparse capabilities row passes — refusing every unverifiable
model would make the screen unusable); the privacy check is based on the
provider's family, which is always populated, so it is unconditional.
llm_provider_admin_router.delete_model now also refuses to delete a model named
by a feature assignment: the FK is ON DELETE SET NULL, so the delete would have
silently blanked the assignment with nothing to show the admin why.

Telemetry lands with this, not after — retrofitting it would mean the
comparison data for the rollout period never exists. model_usages.feature_key
is populated by workers/kafka_consumer._handle_metrics (which covers nine of
the eleven llm_cost producers in one edit), by the AGENTS insert, and by
PostgresMemory.create_model_usage. It cannot go on llm_spend_daily, which holds
provider billing-API data with no feature axis at all.

Also fixes a defect found while wiring that up: agents/sdlc_pipeline/_core.py
called create_model_usage(tokens_in=…, tokens_out=…), which are not parameters
of that function. Every call raised TypeError straight into a bare `except:
pass`, so the SDLC pipeline has never written a single model_usages row — its
spend was invisible in budget and per-department reporting. The handler now
logs instead of swallowing, which is what hid it for as long as it did.

The admin screen keeps four states visually distinct, because they look
identical in a naive dropdown and mean very different things: assigned,
deliberately inheriting, never decided, and pinned on-premise by data
classification. For the last, cloud models are disabled in the dropdown rather
than accepted-then-silently-overridden, and every row shows which precedence
rule produced its current resolution.

77 new tests in tests/core/. All release checks pass — including two that
correctly caught the new sidebar entry being undocumented, now added to
DocsPanel.jsx and the README feature table. No new failures against
scripts/ci/known_failures.txt.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
