From 062dddfefe9a3ad691b52f6db968523b34f3e46e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:20:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] refactor(llm): route 66 call sites through the feature
 resolver, and guard the rest
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 2. Each migrated site is a one-argument substitution, because
ModelRouter.route() step 1a already resolves whatever the resolver returns:

  model_router.generate(prompt, model_hint="complex")
  model_router.generate(prompt, model_hint=resolve_feature_model(
      "skills.generate", default="complex"))

Passing the site's current literal as `default=` makes every one of these
behaviour-preserving until an admin assigns something, which is what let them
land together safely. The ten distinct literals map onto 39 declared features.

Counting matters here, and the first attempt got it wrong. A grep for
`model_hint="` finds 68 occurrences, and a naive rewrite of all 68 mangled:

  * 4 strings of SKILL SOURCE inside routers/skills_router.py's seed templates
    — generated files shipped to users, which do not import the resolver;
  * 3 docstring and comment mentions in agents/doc_generator_agent.py,
    models/doc_intent.py and routers/messages_compat_router.py (prose that
    happens to quote a call);
  * 2 PARAMETER DEFAULTS in agents/sdlc_patch_engine.py, where a resolver call
    is evaluated once at import time and would freeze the admin's assignment
    for the life of the process.

Redone with the AST, matching only genuine `model_hint=<literal>` keywords in
Call nodes. 66 real sites migrated; the 2 parameter defaults are annotated
in place explaining why they stay (their real callers pass
sdlc_stage_hint("coder"), the per-stage SDLC_MODEL_<STAGE> mechanism that is
already provider-agnostic and meant to win). models/doc_intent.py is migrated
too, keeping its existing DOC_INTENT_MODEL env value as the fallback so an
operator's current setting still works.

The new release_checks.py check is a ratchet at 0, matching the existing
model-literals check's shape: the count may fall, never rise, with
skills_router and sdlc_patch_engine allowlisted for the reasons above. It uses
the AST rather than a regex precisely because the regex version is what caused
the mess described above. Negative-tested by reverting one site.

Two features declared in Phase 1b are REMOVED rather than shipped unwired,
with the reason recorded in core/feature_registry.py — a row an admin can set
that has no effect is worse than no row:

  chat.respond  The main conversational answer path routes with NO hint at all
                (route() picks a tier by complexity) and the Chat UI's own
                model picker supplies one when a user chooses. Threading a
                feature default through without overriding the user's explicit
                choice is a design question, not a substitution; it needs
                gateway.py's inline `if tier ==` branches collapsed onto the
                router first, which is deferred with it.
  sdlc.patch    See the parameter-default note above.

Two new tests close the loop in both directions: every declared feature must
be referenced by a call site (no dead admin rows), and every referenced key
must be declared (an undeclared key silently returns the caller's default
forever, so a typo would never surface). Those two tests are what caught
chat.respond and sdlc.patch.

Also moved the resolver import in gateway.py and routers/chat_router.py: the
automated insertion put it after the last top-level import, which in those two
files is thousands of lines below the first use. Valid Python, but it reads as
a mistake. A check confirms no file now imports the resolver after using it.

583 tests passing across tests/{core,agents,config,cil,pipeline}; 19 of the 20
failures are in scripts/ci/known_failures.txt and the 20th fails identically on
an unmodified tree (it asserts a missing JWT_SECRET raises, and the test shell
exports one). All release checks pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
