From 36b6e08d32f713246d88890785be6c24535373e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:24:29 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 05/10] fix(llm): give admin-registered models a circuit
 breaker and a fallback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

TIER_REGISTRY — the path route() takes when it resolves an admin-registered
model_id — was the one tier with neither. Its own source comment said as much:
"No cross-vendor fallback (unlike every other tier above)". An admin-added
model that failed returned an error string straight to the user, while every
built-in tier cascaded across vendors. That made each per-feature assignment
onto an admin-added model a new single point of failure, which is exactly the
risk that made rolling assignment out widely unsafe.

core/circuit_breaker.py needed no change. get_breaker() already accepts an
arbitrary name and falls back to (10, 30) for anything outside
_BREAKER_DEFAULTS, so keying by model id was always possible — the gap was
only that this path never called it. Breakers are keyed per MODEL, not per
family: one dead OpenRouter deployment must not open the breaker for every
other openai_compatible model, nor collide with the built-in
openai/claude/gemini breakers.

Fallback runs in two stages, both marked was_fallback=True so the UI shows
"[fallback]" and the turn is attributed to whichever model actually answered:

  1. The admin's own global default model from the LLM Providers screen, when
     it differs from the one that just failed — a stated operator preference
     beats any built-in assumption.
  2. TIER_MEDIUM, whose own chain is openai -> claude -> local. The most
     resilient built-in cascade, so the right last resort whichever families
     are configured.

This is the RUNTIME failure path. A feature's declared fallback_model_ids are
walked earlier, at resolution time, by core/feature_model_resolver — that
covers "the assigned model was deleted or its provider disabled", which is a
different failure with a different fix.

Two things needed care:

- Gateways signal failure in two different ways (audit §3.8): some raise, some
  RETURN "Error: ...". A breaker only sees the first, so a model that
  persistently returns error strings would never trip. Those are now recorded
  as failures explicitly — and, before this, the string was handed back to the
  user as though it were the model's answer.

- Every gateway's generate() returns a lazy generator, so
  breaker.call(gw.generate, ...) records a success the instant the generator
  object is constructed and never sees the failure that happens while it is
  consumed. The collection now runs inside breaker.call. The built-in tiers
  have the same latent flaw; not touched here, but worth fixing when their
  signatures are normalised in the gateway consolidation phase.

Streaming only decides to fall back before the FIRST token. Once tokens are
flowing the turn is committed — restarting on another model would replay
output the user has already seen — so a mid-stream failure is reported and not
retried. The eagerly-pulled first token is still emitted, in order, exactly
once; there is a test for that specifically.

13 new tests in tests/core/, covering both failure shapes, both fallback
stages, an open breaker short-circuiting without calling the broken model, the
default-model-is-the-broken-model case, and all three streaming paths.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
