From fc6cb13e1a1875653303896b0e1f7f7bd24a913e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:32:32 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 06/10] refactor(llm): collapse the duplicated
 model-classification helpers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 4. The audit counted five byte-identical copies of a cloud-prefix
deny-list and eight mutually-inconsistent "is this model in-house" predicates.
They matter because they decide BILLING: whether a request is budget-checked,
and whether a completed call is priced at zero.

services/endpoint_model_catalog.py already existed for exactly this — its own
header says "rather than add a sixth, this module delegates to the
AUTHORITATIVE source for each question". So this extends it and deletes the
copies rather than adding another.

Budget gates — all five now call one is_budget_exempt():

  middleware/budget_middleware.py, workers/chat_worker.py, gateway.py (twice),
  routers/ide_router.py

Each had the same wrong rule: "not one of seven known cloud prefixes therefore
in-house therefore free". That inverts the safe default — every paid model
whose id starts with none of gpt-/claude-/gemini-/openai//anthropic//google//
azure/ was exempted from budget enforcement entirely, including
mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct, deepseek-chat, o3-mini, Bedrock
us.anthropic.*, Vertex ...@20240620, and every OpenRouter vendor/model id.
classify_model is allowlist-based and fails closed, so an unrecognised model is
now budget-CHECKED. (The middleware copy was fixed in the Phase 0 commit; this
removes the other four and moves the shared implementation into the catalogue
so all five are one function.) routers/messages_compat_router.py's divergent
sixth variant is deliberately untouched — it is a normalisation allowlist, not
a billing gate, and the two are not interchangeable.

Cost estimators — agents/agent_builder.py and routers/projects_router.py now
call the shared estimate_cost_usd() instead of testing for
"local"/"ollama"/"llama" in the name. That test priced genuinely free in-house
models (gemma-4-31B-it, kimi-k2.6, deepseek-v4-flash) at the conservative
unknown-cloud rate, and would price an admin-registered llama-3.1-70b-instruct
served from a PAID endpoint at zero. routers/coach_router.py's _is_local_model
(which feeds _out_cost) delegates the same way.

AgentStudio:
- core/governance.py's _is_local_model — already the best of the eight
  (catalogue first, heuristic second) — now also consults the platform
  provider registry, so a model an admin registered under the ollama family
  is recognised. Those carry neither a "local:" prefix nor any of its
  substrings, so they were previously billed as cloud.
- core/llm_handler.py's "don't send stream_options to local gateways" guard is
  removed: it was dead code. The kwargs dict above it sets stream_options
  unconditionally, so the guard could only re-set a value that was always
  present — local models received it either way.
- cli_runtime/runner.py keeps its static prefix list, because
  _normalize_cli_model is documented as fully synchronous and the catalogue
  reaches Redis/Postgres and the LiteLLM gateway. Its comment claimed to be
  "kept in sync by construction" with governance's heuristic; it is not (it
  adds gemma/deepseek/gpt-oss and omits local/ollama), so the comment now says
  what it actually is and what the bounded consequence is.

Spend tracking — services/llm_spend/approved_models.py now takes llm_models as
a third source alongside the env constants and observed usage. An
admin-registered model was in neither of the first two on the day it was
assigned, so its spend was bucketed as 'other' until it had accumulated 90
days of organic usage: the first weeks on a newly assigned model were
unattributed, which is exactly the window a per-feature assignment needs cost
data for. openai_compatible is bucketed with openai rather than dropped, or
every OpenRouter/Together/vLLM model would land in 'other'.

Also fixes a shipped schema defect found while auditing that path.
llm_spend_daily had NO DDL anywhere in db/migrate.py — db/models.py pointed at
db/sql/prod_catchup_2026_06_17_llm_spend.sql, which is not in this repo — so a
fresh install got whatever create_all() inferred from the ORM class. And the
class disagreed with the live writer: token_type is in _UPSERT_SQL and in its
five-column ON CONFLICT target but was absent from the class, so create_all()
produced a FOUR-column uq_llm_spend_daily and every nightly spend upsert would
fail with "no unique or exclusion constraint matching the ON CONFLICT
specification". Part AD2 adds reproducible, idempotent DDL and repairs an
existing four-column deployment in place (backfilling token_type='blended',
the documented pre-itemisation value). The writer, the ORM and the migration
are now verified to agree on all 11 columns and the 5-column key.

No new test failures. The tests/store/ DB errors in this run reproduce
identically on an unmodified tree — a local Postgres is listening with
credentials these tests do not have.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
