From bf13c9a006fe629edf8b200e0d60a4fe8fefe04a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:37:33 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] feat(llm): serve per-model token limits from the
 registry, widen preferred_model
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Finishes Phase 4's functional items.

modelMaxTokens.js — AgentStudio's Max Tokens control was capped by a static
table keyed by the model ids this project ships with, so every model an
operator registers through the "LLM Providers" screen missed it and silently
received DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS_CAP (4096) plus a wrong context-usage meter. It
already did namespace-stripping, longest-prefix matching and a one-shot console
warning, so the failure mode was under-serving rather than breaking — but an
operator running their own models got a wrong number with no way to correct it.

get_cli_style_models() now carries context_window and max_output_tokens from
llm_models.capabilities, useAvailableModels registers them via a new
registerModelLimits(), and getMaxTokensForModel consults that live map before
the static table. Same "live map first, static table second, derived fallback
last" shape ai-ui/src/components/ModelGovernance.jsx already uses for display
metadata, which keeps the getter synchronous and leaves a deployment that
records no capability metadata behaving exactly as before.

Two properties get their own tests: an entry with no recorded limit is SKIPPED
rather than stored as zero (so an unfilled model still falls through to the
static table), and a registered limit is still clamped to
BACKEND_MAX_TOKENS_LIMIT, so a mis-recorded capability cannot make the UI offer
a value the backend rejects. 17 tests in the first
AgentStudio/frontend/src/utils/__tests__/ directory, matching vitest.config.js's
existing include glob. Assertions were verified against the real module under
node before being committed.

agents_pg.preferred_model — widened from VARCHAR(50) to VARCHAR(255) (Part
AD3). The column holds whatever is passed to route() as model_hint, which since
route() gained registry-model resolution includes concrete model ids of any
provider, and 50 characters is too small for real provider-prefixed ids:
"accounts/fireworks/models/llama-v3p1-70b-instruct" is 49,
"publishers/google/models/gemini-3.5-flash" is 41. Widening a VARCHAR is
metadata-only in PostgreSQL and cannot truncate existing values.

The audit asked for this to become a foreign key to llm_models. It should not
be one, and the reason is now recorded on the column: the domain legitimately
includes NULL/"auto" (no preference), the six capability names, and the legacy
tier aliases — none of which are models — so an FK could not represent it and
there is no safe mapping for the existing values. Referential safety already
comes from the read side instead: route() falls through to complexity
classification for an unrecognised hint, and agent_builder retries with
model_hint=None when the preferred model fails, so a model deleted underneath
an agent degrades rather than breaking it. The old comment claiming the domain
was "auto|claude|gpt|ollama" has not been true for some time and is replaced
with what it actually accepts.

No new test failures; all release checks pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
