From 877c126c3ee419980f21fe92480f0108e1d1b668 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:44:11 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] feat(llm): bring embedding and reranking models into
 the provider registry
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 6's foundation. The registry covered GENERATION only, so an admin could
swap the chat model from a dropdown but had to edit services/embed_svc/.env and
restart a container to change the embedding or reranking model —
"model-agnostic" was not true of retrieval.

llm_models.model_kind (generation | embedding | rerank, Part AD4) makes those
models registerable. Every existing row is generation, so the column is NOT
NULL DEFAULT 'generation' and the backfill is the default. A CHECK rather than
a Postgres ENUM, for the same reason llm_providers.family is a plain VARCHAR:
widening a CHECK is ordinary DDL, while ALTER TYPE on an enum cannot run in a
transaction on older servers. Exposed through the admin API with validation,
and a test asserts the API's accepted values and the CHECK's cannot drift —
a value one accepts and the other rejects is a 500 on save.

The risk this introduces is leakage, and it is the substantive part of the
change: every picker in the platform reads get_enabled_models(), so an
embedding model added to the registry would have appeared in the chat dropdown,
in model governance, and as an assignable feature model. get_enabled_models now
takes kind= and DEFAULTS to "generation", which is what all ~20 existing
callers mean; get_models_by_kind() is the new opt-in. get_model() and
get_model_by_uuid() are generation-scoped too, since they feed route() step 1a
and the feature resolver, both of which send prompts. Rows that predate the
migration (no model_kind at all) still count as generation rather than
vanishing from the pickers.

Changing a model's kind is also guarded: refused while it is the platform
default generation model, or while a feature assignment names it. Either would
silently point a prompt-sending path at a model that cannot answer.

The audit's other §3.19 item needs correcting rather than implementing. It
called services/embed_svc/config.py's `OPENAI_DIMS = 768` a hardcoded value
that "must become a per-model property since dimensionality varies by model".
It is not hardcoded wrongly — 768 is the width of the pgvector COLUMNS the
vectors are stored in, and it was five independent literals that happened to
agree: db/migrate.py's vector(768) in three places, db/models.py's
Vector(768), and this constant, which is sent to OpenAI as the `dimensions:`
truncation parameter precisely so its 1536/3072-wide output fits. Postgres
rejects a vector of the wrong width and a pgvector index built at one width
cannot serve queries at another, so it is a schema invariant. Making it
configurable would corrupt retrieval, not generalise it.

So it is now stated once, as core.config.EMBED_DIM, next to EMBED_PROVIDER
which already carried the same "changing this requires a reindex" warning.
db/models.py and the embed service derive from it, and a test asserts every
vector(N) in the migration matches — a mismatch there means some table stores
vectors the embedder cannot produce.

What this does NOT do, deliberately: apply an embedding-model assignment.
Registering one makes it a CANDIDATE. Every stored vector came from one
specific model and vectors from a different model are not comparable with it
even at the same width, so reassignment is a reindex with an atomic cutover —
a background job over every indexed chunk, with the old index serving until it
completes. Writing that blind, in an environment with no database and no way to
exercise pgvector, would risk silently degrading search rather than changing a
model. The schema, the candidate lists and the dimensionality guard are the
parts that can be built and verified now; the cutover worker is the remaining
piece and needs a running environment. Reranking has no such constraint and is
already switchable.

17 new tests in tests/core/. No new test failures; all release checks pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
