From 8961c99eedd3221b1db2d223e342f5ee7a5a72ce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:40:29 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 09/10] fix(llm): defects found running the feature layer
 against live services
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Everything in the previous eight commits was verified against unit tests and
static analysis only — there was no database, Redis or provider credential
available. With the stack up, `python db/migrate.py` runs, the admin API
answers, and real turns reach Anthropic. Parts AD1-AD4 apply cleanly and are
idempotent across two runs (39/39 features seeded, the 5-column
uq_llm_spend_daily and the model_kind CHECK both confirmed in Postgres; the two
reported failures are ALTER TABLE conversations statement timeouts from lock
contention with the running workers, in parts that predate this work).

Four real defects surfaced that no amount of unit testing would have found,
because each needed either a populated .env or a live Redis.

1. max_data_classification was clobbered on every migration. It is the one
   feature_registry column that is a POLICY decision — which data a feature is
   permitted to see — rather than a description of what the code needs. Part
   AD1's ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE overwrote it from the code declaration, so an
   operator who raised a feature to CONFIDENTIAL silently lost it on the next
   migrate.py run, and with it the on-premise pinning it existed to enforce.
   Verified by round-tripping: set CONFIDENTIAL, re-seed, value survives.
   Now COALESCEd, with a new PUT /feature-models/{key}/classification so it is
   settable at all. That endpoint refuses to raise a feature whose assigned
   model is cloud-served, since the privacy floor would silently override it.

2. An unusable model-id env value was sent upstream verbatim. This .env had
   `CLAUDE_HAIKU=:`. The blank-value fallback in _resolve_tier_model /
   _role_model only triggers on "", so ":" was returned as a model id: every
   haiku/fast turn called the provider with model=":" and model_usages
   recorded a BILLED row with model ':' (in=355 out=3 cost=0.000296), which
   corrupts spend attribution. Both resolvers now treat a value with no
   alphanumeric character as unset, so it degrades to the admin-configured
   registry model exactly as "" already did. Patched in both because
   core/model_registry.py cannot import models/model_router.py — that import
   is circular, and the chain is deliberately duplicated.

3. The same ":" became a _HINT_MAP key. The falsy-key guard strips "" but not
   ":", and such a key can only ever mis-match. Dropped, alongside the
   existing blank-key handling.

4. The registry breaker bypassed CIRCUIT_BREAKER_DISABLED. _try_registry
   pre-checked `breaker.is_open` before calling breaker.call(), but only
   call() honours that kill-switch — so an operator who disabled breakers was
   still short-circuited. The pre-check was also redundant: call() fast-fails
   with RuntimeError before invoking fn when the circuit is OPEN. Removed from
   both the generate and stream paths, and the stream path now opens the stream
   and pulls its first token INSIDE call(), which is where an unreachable model
   actually fails.

Two of my own tests were passing for the wrong reason, both because the
environment was missing:

- test_the_only_new_keys_are_the_capability_names assumed the env-derived
  _HINT_MAP keys were blank, which is true only on an unconfigured checkout. It
  now excludes them by identity, and a new test asserts no _HINT_MAP key is
  unusable as a model id — which is what would have caught defect 3 up front.
- test_registry_model_resilience shared breaker state through REDIS, keyed by
  breaker name, so the failures it deliberately provokes tripped the
  `registry:<model>` breaker and later cases found it already OPEN. It passed
  everywhere Redis was unavailable because is_open fails open. The open-breaker
  case now stubs call() instead of relying on shared state; the suite is stable
  across repeated runs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
