From 8c28b326fc78089040bd2c9330349f693c4534a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anshuman Singh <anshuman.s@npci.org.in>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 15:40:53 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] feat(llm): populate per-feature cost, and make the main
 chat lane assignable
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the two gaps the previous commits left open, both now verified against
the running stack.

PER-FEATURE COST WAS STILL UNANSWERABLE. model_usages.feature_key existed and
workers/kafka_consumer read rec.get("feature_key"), but no producer ever SENT
one — so the column was NULL for every row and "what is Skills generation
costing me on this model versus the alternative?" had no answer. That is the
question the plan said must be answerable from day one, since retrofitting it
means the comparison data for the rollout period never exists.

Rather than thread a parameter through every layer between a call site and its
llm_cost producer, feature_key rides the request context — the same
ContextVar mechanism core/logger.py already uses for request_id, chat_id,
agent_id and pipeline_stage, documented there as asyncio-Task-scoped so FastAPI
gives each request its own copy. core.feature_model_resolver sets it, which
means all ~66 already-migrated call sites get it for free with nothing new to
remember at any of them. It is deliberately NOT added to the emitted log record:
that would change the JSON shape every downstream Loki/promtail query depends on.

The seven producers that serve platform features now read it. The two that do
not are annotated rather than wired: the managed OpenAI-compatible endpoint and
the CLI /v1/messages lane are driven by an external caller choosing its own
model, not by a platform feature, so they must store NULL rather than inherit
whatever feature last ran on the thread.

THE MAIN CHAT LANE IS NOW ASSIGNABLE. chat.respond was removed in the call-site
migration because /ask routes with no hint at all and the Chat picker supplies
one when a user chooses — so a feature default there raised a real question
about whose choice wins, and shipping a row that did nothing would have been
worse than shipping none. With the stack up it could be settled properly:

  * the user's explicit pick (or local_model) always wins;
  * otherwise the admin's chat.respond assignment applies;
  * otherwise nothing changes — chat.respond is declared with
    default_capability=None, so the resolver returns None and the turn
    auto-routes by complexity exactly as before.

So an assignment is opt-in, and the highest-volume lane in the platform finally
answers to the admin screen. Placed before the governance block on purpose: a
hint reaching the router should be access-checked the same way whether a user
or an admin chose it.

Attribution is separate from model choice — set_feature_key("chat.respond")
runs unconditionally. A user overriding the model does not stop the turn being
the chat feature, and setting the key only on the auto-routed branch would have
excluded every picker-selected turn from chat's cost.

Verified end-to-end on the running stack, same trivial prompt each time:

  unassigned            → llama3.2:1b        (auto-routed by complexity)
  assigned sonnet-4-6   → claude-sonnet-4-6  (the admin's choice)
  user picks llama      → llama3.2:1b        (the user's choice wins)

and model_usages carried feature_key='chat.respond' in all three, via a real
Kafka round-trip through the consumer.

Also confirmed live: the resolver reads an assignment written by the admin API
from a DIFFERENT process (so Redis invalidation works), route() step 1a turns
it into TIER_REGISTRY with provider_model_override set, an unassigned feature
still falls through to its call-site literal, and the delete-model,
capability-name, mutual-exclusion and privacy-floor guards all reject as
designed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
